# tool-versions.env — the scanner versions this repo scans with.
#
# One place to bump a tool. Read by tool-runner.sh; every runner and both pipelines pick up a
# change here with no other edit.
#
# TAGS VS DIGESTS
#   Tags are readable and fine while iterating. Before an accreditation run, replace each with a
#   digest so a scan is exactly reproducible:
#     docker buildx imagetools inspect <ref> --format '{{.Manifest.Digest}}'
#
#   ClamAV is the exception and must STAY on a moving tag. Grype and Trivy download their
#   vulnerability data at run time, so pinning them says nothing about data freshness. ClamAV's
#   signatures ship inside its image, so a digest pin freezes the signatures permanently and the
#   scan quietly stops recognising anything published after that build.
#
#   That rule covers patch tags too, not just digests. This file read clamav/clamav:1.4.1 until
#   2026-09-19; that tag has never been published — the 1.4 line is at 1.4.6 — so `docker run`
#   failed to resolve it and exited 125 before any container started. The published moving tags
#   are `stable`, `latest`, and the minor lines (`1.4`, `1.5`); the patch tags exist but are
#   rebuilt once and then never again, which is a digest pin wearing a tag's clothes.
#
#   A pin that is too old freezes the data too. Grype reads only the database schema it was built
#   for, and Anchore stops publishing an old schema: v0.87.0 still downloaded a database on
#   2026-10-15, but one built 2026-03-09, seven months stale, and the scan reported it as current.
#   The Scan gate now fails a CVE database older than the profile allows (scanners/securebuilderbot_scanners/
#   gate.py), so a pin that falls behind its schema shows up as a red gate, not a quiet pass.

SCAN_IMAGE_HADOLINT="${SCAN_IMAGE_HADOLINT:-hadolint/hadolint:v2.12.0-alpine}"
SCAN_IMAGE_CHECKOV="${SCAN_IMAGE_CHECKOV:-bridgecrew/checkov:3.2.334}"
SCAN_IMAGE_CLAMAV="${SCAN_IMAGE_CLAMAV:-clamav/clamav:stable}"
SCAN_IMAGE_GRYPE="${SCAN_IMAGE_GRYPE:-anchore/grype:v0.119.0}"
SCAN_IMAGE_TRIVY="${SCAN_IMAGE_TRIVY:-aquasec/trivy:0.58.2}"

# PLATFORM
#   clamav/clamav publishes linux/amd64 only — no tag carries an arm64 manifest. On an arm64 host
#   (Apple Silicon, arm64 CI) `docker run` with no --platform still runs the image under emulation,
#   but first writes
#     WARNING: The requested image's platform (linux/amd64) does not match the detected host...
#   into the command's own output. That line lands in the string the signature-freshness guard
#   parses for a build date; the parse finds no date and the guard blocks the scan as UNVERIFIED —
#   a correct refusal to an input that should never have reached it. Naming the platform silences
#   the warning and the guard reads a real version line. On an amd64 host this is a no-op.
#
#   This is not the 2026-09-19 exit 125, which was an unpublished tag. That failure was in CI
#   (amd64) and this one never is, which is why it outlived four ClamAV fixes: GitHub runners are
#   amd64 and every developer Mac since 2020 is not.
#
#   The other four scanners publish arm64 and are left to resolve natively — forcing emulation on
#   them would be slower for nothing. Set empty to opt out.
#   Note the `-` without a colon, unlike every image above. `:-` substitutes on empty as well as
#   unset, which would make `SCAN_PLATFORM_CLAMAV=` silently fall back to the default and turn the
#   documented opt-out into a no-op. An empty image ref is meaningless so `:-` is right for those;
#   an empty platform is meaningful — it means omit the flag.
SCAN_PLATFORM_CLAMAV="${SCAN_PLATFORM_CLAMAV-linux/amd64}"

# PIP-AUDIT
#   The ecosystem audit for Python apps runs natively (`python3 -m pip_audit`): on a developer
#   machine from scanners/requirements.txt, in CI from the same file, in the scanners image from
#   its Dockerfile. All three install this exact version, and run-pip-audit.sh refuses any other
#   so a scan is the pinned tool or no scan. Its vulnerability data (PyPI's advisory feed) is read
#   live at run time, like npm audit's.
SCAN_PIP_AUDIT_VERSION="${SCAN_PIP_AUDIT_VERSION:-2.10.1}"
